  "versions": [
    {
      "summary": "Aaron was Moses' older brother and Israel's first high priest. He served as Moses' spokesman before Pharaoh and led the people in worship. Although he failed by making the golden calf, he and his descendants were appointed to the priesthood forever.",
      "title": "Israel's first high priest",
      "score": 5,
      "reason": "[major] Aaron had several important roles beyond just 'priest' and 'leader'. He was specifically high priest, prophet (Exod 7:1), and spokesperson for Moses. The roles should be more precise and complete.; [minor] The event titled 'For Pharaoh' has a somewhat imprecise title. It should be expanded to, for example, 'Aaron speaks before Pharaoh' or 'Aaron's staff before Pharaoh' for clarity.; [major] A key event is missing: Aaron's death on Mount Hor (Num 20:22-29), where his high priestly garments were transferred to his son Eleazar. This is a central concluding event in Aaron's life.; [minor] The event where Aaron stopped the plague by standing between the living and the dead with incense (Num 17:11-13 / 16:46-48 in some numbering systems) is an important typological moment that demonstrates his mediatorial role.; [minor] The summary is good but could be expanded slightly. It could mention that Aaron belonged to the tribe of Levi and that he is also described as a prophet (Exod 7:1). The phrase 'priesthood forever' could be clarified.; [major] There are no footnotes. A profile on Aaron should include footnotes covering historical, linguistic, and theological aspects of his life and service.; [minor] The lifespan 'c. 1530-1407 BC' is reasonable within an early dating of the Exodus, but it should be noted that this is debated. The Bible states that Aaron was 123 years old when he died (Num 33:39).",
      "date": "2026-04-05"
    },
    {
      "summary": "Aaron was Moses' older brother from the tribe of Levi and became Israel's first high priest. God appointed him as prophet and spokesman for Moses before Pharaoh during the events of the Exodus from Egypt. Aaron led the people in worship and sacrificial rituals in the tabernacle. Despite his failure with the golden calf, God confirmed his calling by making his staff blossom. Aaron and his descendants were installed into the priesthood as a permanent arrangement, and the Aaronic priesthood pointed forward to a greater high priest who was to come.",
      "title": "Israel's first high priest and Moses' spokesperson",
      "score": 7,
      "reason": "[major] The event \"Aron stops the plague\" refers to Num 17:11-13, but in most Hebrew and Norwegian translations this event is described in Num 17:11-13 (which corresponds to 16:46-48 in some verse numbering systems). However, it is more important to note that verses 11-13 in chapter 17 overlap with the verses used in the previous event (\"The Staff That Budded\"). The plague that Aron stops with the censer actually occurs in Num 17:11-13 in Hebrew numbering, but in many translations this is Num 16:46-48. It is confusing that two different events share exactly the same verse references (17:11). \"Aron stops the plague\" should refer to chapter 17 verses 11-13 (Hebrew numbering) or chapter 16 verses 46-48 (other numberings). For consistency, this should be clarified.; [critical] The event of Aron stopping the plague occurred during Korah's rebellion and is described in Num 16:46-48 (in many translations) or Num 17:11-13 in the Hebrew text. The problem is that \"The Staff That Budded\" already uses chapter 17 verses 8-11, and \"Aron stops the plague\" uses 17:11-13 – verse 11 overlaps. The plague with the censer most correctly belongs to Num 17:11-13 in Hebrew numbering, but to avoid confusion with the previous event one should use the alternative numbering (16:46-48) or clarify that these are different verses.; [minor] The sentence \"the Aaronic priesthood pointed forward to a greater high priest who was to come\" uses the word \"high priest\" while the rest of the text uses \"chief priest\". The terminology should be consistent throughout the profile.; [minor] Aron also had a role as an intercessor/mediator (as illustrated in the event where he stops the plague by standing between the living and the dead). One could consider adding \"intercessor\" or \"mediator\" as a role.",
      "date": "2026-04-05"
    }
  ],
  "footnotes": [
    {
      "text": "The name Aaron (Hebrew: אַהֲרֹן, Aharon) has an uncertain etymology. Some scholars have suggested a connection to the Egyptian word 'aha rw' (great warrior) or the Hebrew 'har' (mountain), while others believe it means 'enlightened' or 'exalted'. The Egyptian connection is interesting given the context of Israel in Egypt.",
      "source": "lingvistisk"
    },
    {
      "text": "Aaron's role as high priest established the Aaronic priesthood, which continued throughout the entire Old Testament period until the destruction of the temple in 70 AD. In the Epistle to the Hebrews (Heb 5:1–4; 7:11), the Aaronic priesthood is used as a contrast to Christ's priesthood according to the order of Melchizedek, emphasizing that the Aaronic priesthood was temporary and pointed toward a perfect fulfillment.",
      "source": "teologisk"
    },
    {
      "text": "The consecration ritual for Aaron and his sons (Lev 8) lasted seven days and included washing, dressing in holy garments, anointing with oil, and several sacrifices. The number seven symbolizes completeness in biblical tradition, and the ritual emphasized that priestly service required thorough sanctification.",
      "source": "teologisk"
    },
    {
      "text": "In rabbinic tradition (Pirke Avot 1:12), Aaron is highlighted as a model of peacemaking and love for people. He is described as one who actively sought to reconcile conflicting parties, which was seen as just as important as his priestly function.",
      "source": "rabbinsk"
    },
    {
      "text": "Archaeological finds from the Second Temple period, including inscriptions mentioning 'Aaron's sons', confirm that the genealogy of the Aaronic priesthood was carefully maintained. Such findings illustrate the continued significance of the priesthood throughout both the biblical and post-biblical periods.",
      "source": "arkeologisk"
    },
    {
      "text": "Aaron's staff that budded and bore almonds (Num 17) was, in Jewish tradition, kept before or inside the Ark of the Covenant as a testimony. The almond (Hebrew: שָׁקֵד, shaqed) has a wordplay connection with the verb 'to watch' (שָׁקַד, shaqad), emphasizing God's vigilance over His chosen priesthood.",
      "source": "lingvistisk"
    },
    {
      "text": "Mount Hor, where Aaron died, is traditionally identified with Jebel Harun (Aaron's Mountain) near Petra in present-day Jordan. At the summit stands a mosque from the 14th century that, according to tradition, marks Aaron's burial place. The site has been a pilgrimage destination for Jews, Christians, and Muslims for centuries.",
      "source": "historisk"
    }
  ]
}
